\newpage
\section{Overview of the Galaxy Rendering Pipeline}

\texttt{galaxy\_cli.py} provides a programmable, chain–driven point-cloud 
renderer for producing high–resolution black–on–white (or inverted) galaxy images.  
A ``chain'' is a comma–separated sequence of operations, each with optional 
arguments, interpreted by the \texttt{specparser}/\texttt{expandspec} system.  
Each operation transforms an evolving complex vector 
\(\,z \in \mathbb{C}^N\,\) together with an auxiliary ``dot–multiplier’’
state that controls per–point radii during rasterization.

The pipeline has three layers:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Point generators}: create raw point sets 
        (e.g.\ \texttt{rud}, \texttt{rua}, \texttt{runif}, 
        triangular/square samplers, IFS fractals, Hilbert/Moore/Z–curves,
        Lorenz/Clifford/Hénon/DeJong/Tinker/Gumira/Peano/Vicsek, etc.).
        These append new points to the tail of~\(z\).

  \item \textbf{Geometric transforms}: smooth, swirl, warp, reflect, discretize,
        pixelize, diffuse, or apply kaleidoscopic transforms to the unfrozen tail.  
        All transforms operate either on Cartesian coordinates 
        \(x=\Re z\), \(y=\Im z\), or on radius–angle decompositions.
        Examples include:
        \begin{itemize}
          \item \textit{teardrop deformation} (\texttt{td})
          \item \textit{global or local swirl} (\texttt{swirl})
          \item \textit{arms} (radial duplication)
          \item \texttt{walk}/\texttt{pwalk}/\texttt{ddiffuse}
          \item clipping primitives (\texttt{clp*})
          \item discretizers and pixelizers (\texttt{idscr}, \texttt{rdscr}, 
                \texttt{ddscr}, \texttt{pixelate}, \texttt{xorelate}, 
                \texttt{equalate})
        \end{itemize}

  \item \textbf{Dot–size groups}: each call to \texttt{dot}, \texttt{ldot}, or 
        other dot–operations ``freezes'' the current tail and writes a new 
        vector of per–point radii into the \texttt{K\_MULT} state.  
        Subsequent operations modify only the new tail, enabling multi-layer 
        compositions where different subsets of points receive different 
        effective pixel radii during rasterization.
        Group IDs are stored in the imaginary part of the state vector.
\end{enumerate}

The result of evaluating a chain is the tuple
\[
   (z,\; \mathrm{mult}),
\]
where \(\mathrm{mult}_i \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}\) denotes the logical radius of 
point~\(z_i\), later converted to pixel units by
\[
   r_{\mathrm{px}} = \mathrm{mult} \cdot \texttt{fos}\cdot (\texttt{pix}-1).
\]

The final image is obtained by projecting \(z\) into pixel coordinates with a 
margin fraction and stamping filled discs of radius \(r_{\mathrm{px}}\) onto a 
bilevel canvas.

\newpage
\section{Chain Evaluation and Rendering}

A chain is a sequence
\[
   \texttt{name\_1:arg\_1, \quad name\_2:arg\_2, \quad \dots}
\]
expanded by \texttt{expandspec} into one or more fully–specified chains.
Each chain is evaluated by the macro engine:

\[
   z_0 = \varnothing,\qquad
   (z_{k+1},\, \text{state}) = 
      \texttt{op}_{k}(z_k, a_k, \text{state}).
\]

All operations belong to the registry \texttt{ALLOWED}.  
Generators append points; transforms mutate the unfrozen tail;  
size–ops write to the dot–multiplier state.

\paragraph{Freezing semantics.}
Whenever a dot–operation (\texttt{dot}, \texttt{ldot}, \texttt{dotset}, \texttt{dcopy}, \ldots)
runs, the current tail of \(z\) is assigned a new group ID.  
Future geometric operations modify only points added \emph{after} that group.  
Thus a chain naturally decomposes into ``generative stages,’’ 
each associated with its dot–size profile.

\paragraph{Projection and rasterization.}
Given \(z\) and \(\mathrm{mult}\),
the system performs:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Logical projection}:
        \[
            (x_i,y_i) = \text{scaled and centered coordinates of } z_i,
        \]
        with a margin fraction to avoid boundary clipping.

  \item \textbf{Radius bucketing}:  
        integer radii \(r_{\mathrm{px}} \in \mathbb{N}\) are grouped by 
        radius value.  
        A parallel bucketing kernel (\texttt{bucket\_by\_radius\_parallel}) 
        reorders indices so that all points of the same radius are contiguous.

  \item \textbf{Stamping}:  
        for each radius \(r\), precomputed offsets
        \(\{(\Delta x,\Delta y)\}\) form a filled disc.  
        Batches of points are stamped into a 
        \texttt{pix}\(\times\)\texttt{pix}  
        uint8 canvas.  
        This produces a bilevel image with anti–aliased edge appearance due to 
        the dense point structure, not due to greyscale blending.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Mosaic mode.}
If a chain expands to multiple variants, each variant is rendered as an 
independent tile.  
The \texttt{mosaic} function arranges tiles in a regular grid with optional 
gap, per–tile footer text, and optional thumbnail export.

\paragraph{Determinism.}
All Numba kernels use fixed RNG seeds at the Python level, and operations such 
as \texttt{\_rmax\_serial} ensure radius–dependent transforms remain 
deterministic across parallel hardware.

\newpage
\section{Point Generators}

Point generators append new complex points to the current tail of \(z\).
They do not modify previously frozen groups and they do not interact with the
dot–multiplier state except through the chain structure.

Generators fall into three categories:

\subsection*{1. Random generators}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{runif}: uniform in a rectangle.
  \item \texttt{rnorm}: Gaussian cloud.
  \item \texttt{rud}: radial distribution in a disk, using \(r = u^{d_\theta}\).
  \item \texttt{rua}: arc segment between two angles.
  \item \texttt{rus}: square with exponent distortion.
  \item \texttt{rtr}, \texttt{rtrd}: triangle distributions (uniform or Dirichlet).
  \item \texttt{rsqd}, \texttt{rsqedge}: square and square-edge distribution.
\end{itemize}

All these calls produce
\[
   z_{\mathrm{new}} = \{x_i + i y_i\}_{i=1}^{N},
\]
with the shape of the distribution determined by geometric parameters.

\subsection*{2. Deterministic grids}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{serp}: serpentine raster scan over a \(m\times m\) mesh.
  \item \texttt{carusq}, \texttt{cargsq}: Cartesian square sampling 
        (uniform or grid).
  \item \texttt{carudsk}, \texttt{cargdsk}: Cartesian disk sampling
        (uniform or ring-discretized).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3. Fractal and chaotic generators}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Lorenz, Clifford, Hénon, DeJong, Tinker, Gumira maps.
  \item Hilbert, Moore, Z–curve, Peano, Peano–poly, Vicsek, Gosper (“flowsnake”).
  \item Levy C–curve, Koch snowflake, Heighway dragon.
  \item IFS fractals: \texttt{sierptri}, \texttt{sierpcarpet}, \texttt{fern}.
  \item Julia set sampler: \texttt{julia}.
\end{itemize}

These provide structured or chaotic point clouds suitable for later
transformations.

\newpage
\section{Geometric Transforms}

Geometric transforms mutate the current tail of \(z\) in place.  
All transforms operate on \(x=\Re z\) and \(y=\Im z\) through either 
direct coordinate manipulation or pointwise functions.

\subsection*{Continuous deformations}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{td}: teardrop deformation with smoothstep blending.
  \item \texttt{swirl}: radial swirl, applied only where \(|z| > 1\).
  \item \texttt{squish}: vertical squashing.
  \item \texttt{arms}: rotational replication into \(m\) arms.
  \item \texttt{rot}, \texttt{grot}, \texttt{sqrot}: rotations and
        elliptical shears.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Random diffusion}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{walk}: uniform random walk.
  \item \texttt{pwalk}: amplitude–weighted walk.
  \item \texttt{ddiffuse}: radial diffusion.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Kaleidoscope transforms}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{elkal}, \texttt{elkal1}: ellipse–based ring structures.
  \item \texttt{linkal}: line–based kaleidoscope warped by ring index.
  \item \texttt{asp}: Archimedean spiral based on real seed.
  \item \texttt{lsp}: logarithmic spiral.
\end{itemize}

All transforms preserve group boundaries (freezing semantics).

\newpage
\section{Clipping and Filtering}

Clipping operations remove points from the unfrozen tail based on geometric
predicate tests.  

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{clpindisk}, \texttt{clpoutdisk}: inside/outside disks.
  \item \texttt{clpinsq}, \texttt{clpoutsq}: inside/outside squares.
  \item \texttt{clpinrect}, \texttt{clpoutrect}: rectangular windows.
\end{itemize}

These return
\[
   z' = \{ z_i \in \text{tail} : \text{predicate}(z_i) \}.
\]

Composite macros (e.g.\ \texttt{frame}, \texttt{yin}) chain multiple clipping
operations to sculpt complex outlines from an initially large sample.

\newpage
\section{Discretizers and Pixelization}

These operators map continuous point clouds onto piecewise–constant grids,
useful for producing ``mosaic,'' ``pixel,'' or ``banded’’ structures.

\subsection*{Coordinate–based discretizers}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{discr}, \texttt{rdscr}: quantize one axis and apply a power.
  \item \texttt{idscr}: horizontal line discretization with boundary preservation.
  \item \texttt{ddscr}: radial discretization in polar coordinates.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Histogram–based pixel operations}

Using \(\texttt{histogram2d}\), bins are populated and converted back into 
representative centers.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{pixelate}: retain bins with count \(\ge\) threshold.
  \item \texttt{xorelate}: retain bins with count mod \(k \ne 0\).
  \item \texttt{equalate}: retain bins with count \(=k\).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Stochastic pixelization}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{gpixsq}, \texttt{gpixdsk}: gradual pixelization with 
        smoothstep–weighted replacement inside each bin.
\end{itemize}

These create structural abstractions and “digital” textures when fed into
later transforms.

\newpage
\section{Dot--Size Groups}

Dot–size operations control the rasterized radius of each point.  
They do not change coordinates; instead they write size vectors into the 
\texttt{K\_MULT} state.

\subsection*{Creating groups}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{dot}: constant logical size.
  \item \texttt{ldot}: lognormal size distribution.
\end{itemize}

Each call freezes the current tail and creates a new group:
\[
   \mathrm{mult}[i] = s_i + i \cdot \mathrm{gid},
\]
where the real part gives the logical radius and the imaginary part encodes
the group ID.

\subsection*{Modifying existing groups}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{dotset}: overwrite last group’s sizes.
  \item \texttt{dotrhset}, \texttt{dotulset}, \texttt{dotlrset}: 
        modify sizes based on quadrant selection.
  \item \texttt{doturln}, \texttt{dotllln}: local lognormal replacements.
  \item \texttt{dcopy}: replicate the size distribution of an earlier group.
  \item \texttt{dneg}: sign–flip the last group's sizes.
\end{itemize}

Groups partition the evolution of the chain into explicit layers.  
When the image is rasterized, each layer’s size controls the disk radius
assigned to its points.

\newpage
\section{Numba Kernels and Performance}

Many core operations are implemented using \texttt{Numba} for speed.  
The system distinguishes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Serial kernels}: deterministic reductions (e.g.\ radius maxima) 
        where order must be preserved.
  \item \textbf{Parallel kernels}: per–point transforms (\texttt{swirl},
        \texttt{td}, \texttt{\_sink\_gaussian\_inplace\_fast}, \ldots).
  \item \textbf{Hybrid kernels}: serial precomputation followed by parallel
        per–point application.
\end{itemize}

The geometry warmup routine pre-binds ufunc loops to avoid JIT latency during
the first large render.

All random generators rely on a Python–level \texttt{default\_rng} for 
reproducibility.  
Parallelism is confined to pointwise kernels; deterministic components 
(e.g.\ global radius estimation) remain serial to avoid race–induced variation.



